ng the Room Open visual","loading":"eager","decoding":"async"},"children":[]},"\n              "]},"\n\n              ",{"tag":"div","attributes":{"className":"crays-nostr-hero-signal"},"children":["\n                ",{"tag":"span","attributes":{},"children":["People"]},"\n                ",{"tag":"strong","attributes":{},"children":["Infrastructure"]},"\n                ",{"tag":"small","attributes":{},"children":["Use this when you want the people behind uptime, policy, paid relays, archives, spam defense and community infrastructure."]},"\n              "]},"\n              ",{"tag":"div","attributes":{"className":"crays-nostr-hero-pins"},"children":["\n                ",{"tag":"a","attributes":{"href":"/nostr/media/creators/"},"children":["Creators"]},"\n",{"tag":"a","attributes":{"href":"/nostr/people/events/"},"children":["Events"]},"\n",{"tag":"a","attributes":{"href":"/nostr/media/music-video-media/"},"children":["Music"]},"\n              "]},"\n            "]},"\n\n        "]},"\n      "]},"\n\n\n\n\n\n      ",{"tag":"section","attributes":{"className":"crays-article-main"},"children":["\n        ",{"tag":"div","attributes":{"className":"crays-article-reader-shell crays-article-layout"},"children":["\n          ",{"tag":"aside","attributes":{"className":"crays-article-sidebar"},"children":["\n            ",{"tag":"a","attributes":{"className":"crays-article-back","href":"/nostr/people/"},"children":["Back to People"]},"\n            ",{"tag":"nav","attributes":{"className":"crays-article-toc","aria-label":"Article sections"},"children":["\n            ",{"tag":"strong","attributes":{},"children":["On this page"]},"\n            ",{"tag":"a","attributes":{"href":"#a-relay-is-never-only-a-pipe"},"children":[{"tag":"span","attributes":{},"children":["01"]},{"tag":"strong","attributes":{},"children":["A relay is never only a pipe"]}]},"\n            ",{"tag":"a","attributes":{"href":"#paid-relays-changed-the-tone-of-the-debate"},"children":[{"tag":"span","attributes":{},"children":["02"]},{"tag":"strong","attributes":{},"children":["Paid relays changed the tone of the debate"]}]},"\n            ",{"tag":"a","attributes":{"href":"#relay-lists-make-the-hidden-layer-visible"},"children":[{"tag":"span","attributes":{},"children":["03"]},{"tag":"strong","attributes":{},"children":["Relay lists make the hidden layer visible"]}]},"\n            ",{"tag":"a","attributes":{"href":"#relay-operators-are-the-quiet-hosts-of-the-party"},"children":[{"tag":"span","attributes":{},"children":["04"]},{"tag":"strong","attributes":{},"children":["Relay operators are the quiet hosts of the party"]}]},"\n            ",{"tag":"a","attributes":{"href":"#web-of-trust-and-paid-relays-make-the-politics-visible"},"children":[{"tag":"span","attributes":{},"children":["05"]},{"tag":"strong","attributes":{},"children":["Web of trust and paid relays make the politics visible"]}]},"\n            ",{"tag":"a","attributes":{"href":"#the-relay-story-is-the-maturity-test"},"children":[{"tag":"span","attributes":{},"children":["06"]},{"tag":"strong","attributes":{},"children":["The relay story is the maturity test"]}]},"\n            ",{"tag":"a","attributes":{"href":"#sources"},"children":[{"tag":"span","attributes":{},"children":["07"]},{"tag":"strong","attributes":{},"children":["Sources worth opening"]}]},"\n            "]},"\n          "]},"\n          ",{"tag":"div","attributes":{"className":"crays-article-content"},"children":["\n            ",{"tag":"header","attributes":{"className":"crays-article-header"},"children":["\n              ",{"tag":"div","attributes":{"className":"crays-article-meta"},"children":["\n                ",{"tag":"span","attributes":{},"children":["People"]},"\n                ",{"tag":"span","attributes":{},"children":["7 min read"]},"\n                ",{"tag":"span","attributes":{},"children":["Infrastructure"]},"\n              "]},"\n              ",{"tag":"h2","attributes":{},"children":["Relay Operators: The Quiet People Holding the Room Open"]},"\n              ",{"tag":"p","attributes":{},"children":["Relays are easy to describe as servers and hard to understand as social institutions. Their operators make choices that affect reach, memory, spam, privacy and who feels welcome."]},"\n            "]},"\n\n            ",{"tag":"div","attributes":{"className":"crays-article-callout"},"children":["\n              ",{"tag":"strong","attributes":{},"children":["Reader route:"]}," Use this when you want the people behind uptime, policy, paid relays, archives, spam defense and community infrastructure.\n            "]},"\n\n          ",{"tag":"section","attributes":{"className":"crays-article-section","id":"a-relay-is-never-only-a-pipe"},"children":["\n            ",{"tag":"h3","attributes":{},"children":["A relay is never only a pipe"]},"\n            ",{"tag":"p","attributes":{},"children":["A relay can accept, store and serve signed events. That sounds neutral until the operator has to decide what to store, how long to keep it, who can publish, what to rate-limit, whether to charge, how to handle spam and whether the relay exists for everyone or for a specific community. Suddenly the server has a social role. It decides which parts of the room remain reachable."]},"\n            ",{"tag":"p","attributes":{},"children":["Relay operators often stay less visible than client makers, but the network depends on them. If relays are unreliable, the user thinks Nostr is broken. If relays are flooded, the user thinks Nostr is hostile. If relays are too closed, the user thinks Nostr is a club. Infrastructure becomes culture through these choices."]},"\n          "]},"\n\n          ",{"tag":"section","attributes":{"className":"crays-article-section","id":"paid-relays-changed-the-tone-of-the-debate"},"children":["\n            ",{"tag":"h3","attributes":{},"children":["Paid relays changed the tone of the debate"]},"\n            ",{"tag":"p","attributes":{},"children":["Paid relays are one of Nostr's clearest examples of social tradeoff. Charging can reduce spam, create accountability and pay for infrastructure. It can also create barriers and fragment discovery. The debate is not only technical. It asks what kind of public square people want: completely open, lightly gated, community-specific, market-priced or personally curated."]},"\n            ",{"tag":"p","attributes":{},"children":["A People article about relay operators needs to make that tradeoff visible. The operator is not just running a WebSocket endpoint. They are choosing a posture toward openness, abuse, sustainability and user trust."]},"\n          "]},"\n\n          ",{"tag":"section","attributes":{"className":"crays-article-section","id":"relay-lists-make-the-hidden-layer-visible"},"children":["\n            ",{"tag":"h3","attributes":{},"children":["Relay lists make the hidden layer visible"]},"\n            ",{"tag":"p","attributes":{},"children":["NIP-65 and relay-list behavior help expose where an account expects to be found. That is valuable because relay choice can otherwise disappear beneath the client. A reader who understands relay lists can better understand why an account loads in one place and not another, why replies are missing or why a community feels scattered."]},"\n            ",{"tag":"p","attributes":{},"children":["This is also why relay-aware clients and relay explorers matter. They give users a way to see the infrastructure under the conversation. The People map can then connect operators, tools and public accounts into one readable trail."]},"\n          "]},"\n\n          ",{"tag":"section","attributes":{"className":"crays-article-section","id":"relay-operators-are-the-quiet-hosts-of-the-party"},"children":["\n            ",{"tag":"h3","attributes":{},"children":["Relay operators are the quiet hosts of the party"]},"\n            ",{"tag":"p","attributes":{},"children":["A relay operator rarely becomes the public face of Nostr, but the whole room depends on them. A relay can be open, paid, filtered, archival, community-specific, wallet-oriented, spam-resistant or experimental. Those choices determine whether a user finds old posts, sees replies, avoids floods, joins a community or gives up because nothing loads. Infrastructure becomes social behavior."]},"\n            ",{"tag":"p","attributes":{},"children":["This is why relay people belong in the People map. They are closer to hosts than pipes. A host decides whether the door is open, whether the room is noisy, whether people can stay, whether abuse is tolerated and whether the cost of keeping the lights on is shared. Relays do not own identity, but they deeply shape experience."]},"\n            ",{"tag":"p","attributes":{},"children":["The reader should learn to see a relay operator as a public actor in the network, not only a server admin."]},"\n          "]},"\n\n          ",{"tag":"section","attributes":{"className":"crays-article-section","id":"web-of-trust-and-paid-relays-make-the-politics-visible"},"children":["\n            ",{"tag":"h3","attributes":{},"children":["Web of trust and paid relays make the politics visible"]},"\n            ",{"tag":"p","attributes":{},"children":["Web-of-trust relays and paid relays make the hidden politics of Nostr easier to see. If a relay filters by social graph, it is making a bet that trust can travel through relationships. If a relay charges, it is making a bet that cost can reduce abuse and pay for infrastructure. If a relay stays fully open, it is making a bet that clients and users can handle the mess. None of these bets is neutral."]},"\n            ",{"tag":"p","attributes":{},"children":["Coracle, wot.nostr.net, NIP-65 relay lists and relay-aware clients help readers understand why the same Nostr identity can feel different in different rooms. The protocol lets users carry a key; the relays decide which parts of the public life remain reachable. That is the quiet drama of Nostr infrastructure."]},"\n            ",{"tag":"p","attributes":{},"children":["Relay writing should make that drama legible without turning it into server jargon."]},"\n          "]},"\n\n          ",{"tag":"section","attributes":{"className":"crays-article-section","id":"the-relay-story-is-the-maturity-test"},"children":["\n            ",{"tag":"h3","attributes":{},"children":["The relay story is the maturity test"]},"\n            ",{"tag":"p","attributes":{},"children":["A young protocol can survive on enthusiasm for a while. A real public network needs infrastructure that remains available when the novelty fades. Relay operators, monitor builders, paid infrastructure teams and community relay maintainers are part of that maturity test. Their work is often invisible until it fails."]},"\n            ",{"tag":"p","attributes":{},"children":["The archive has to make invisible work visible. Not with flattery, but with context: which relays serve which communities, which tools help users understand relay health and which operators have become trusted parts of the scene."]},"\n          "]},"\n\n            ",{"tag":"section","attributes":{"className":"crays-article-section crays-article-sources","id":"sources"},"children":["\n              ",{"tag":"h3","attributes":{},"children":["Sources worth opening"]},"\n              ",{"tag":"ul","attributes":{},"children":["\n",{"tag":"li","attributes":{},"children":[{"tag":"a","attributes":{"href":"https://github.com/nostr-protocol/nips/blob/master/65.md","target":"_blank","rel":"noopener"},"children":["NIP-65"]}," - Relay list metadata, important when checking whether an account is reachable."]},"\n",{"tag":"li","attributes":{},"children":[{"tag":"a","attributes":{"href":"https://nostr.org/","target":"_blank","rel":"noopener"},"children":["Nostr.org"]}," - Official protocol entry point and mental model for clients, relays and user-controlled identity."]},"\n",{"tag":"li","attributes":{},"children":[{"tag":"a","attributes":{"href":"https://nostr.how/","target":"_blank",